Use semantic icon colours to select the right role for the active brand and light/dark mode. For example, `color="hero"` selects `icon-hero`, which can differ from `surface-hero` in dark mode. Use `mono` or `reversed` on an appropriate contrasting background.

Omit `color` to inherit the parent's foreground, including button and link states. Older colours without an icon-token equivalent, such as `pop`, `muted-vivid` and faint variants, remain supported with their previous surface/background mappings. Prefer semantic roles for new usage.

```tsx
() => {
  const ALL_COLORS = [
    'body',
    'heading',
    'muted',
    'primary',
    'hero',
    'holler',
    'link',
    'success',
    'info',
    'warning',
    'danger',
    'system-error',
    'mono',
    'reversed',
  ] as const;

  return (
    <div className="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-2">
      {ALL_COLORS.map(color => (
        <div key={color} className="flex flex-col items-center gap-1 w-14">
          <div className={color === 'mono' || color === 'reversed' ? 'bg-surface-hero p-1' : 'p-1'}>
            <TickCircleIcon color={color} />
          </div>
          <p className="typography-body-11 text-text-body text-center">{color}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};
```
